 japan is right near the  bottom along with  generally other asian countries  the average score is like  500 or something or less 400 something  which is a very low level in my opinion  and when you are at that level you  aren't going to be able to speak well  period  and  um  toeic results basically reflect your  vocabulary in my opinion your vocabulary  and your ability to read and your  ability to understand when you're  listening  so  if those japanese learners want to speak  better they first of all have to upgrade  their listening and reading capabilities  a little further on this subject  uh you know before we had writing  which is long before we had mb mp3  players of course you could only learn  the language by speaking with someone  and while speaking to someone you would  listen to them and you would slowly  learn the language then writing was  invented and writing is a way of  recording the language  so that without being face to face with  a native speaker you can now interact  with the language and you can acquire  words and phrases and become familiar  with the language  now with the advent of  you know audio recorders and now very  convenient you know mp3 technology  you can read something and then listen  to it and listening to it  is very powerful and it's interesting in  analyzing these toeic results  that when they said okay what correlates  to success on toeic  the correlation to vocabulary level  uh on the reading part of toeic toeic is  by the way test of english  i don't know something it's a very  common test for those of you not  familiar with toeic it's a very common  and i think quite a good test less  expensive than toefl which establishes  your level in the language it's used by  employers it's used by  other people to determine roughly where  you are in the language  it's a big industry millions of people  worldwide take toeic they found that  your vocabulary correlates very closely  to the results on the written like the  reading portion of toeic  however correlates correlates less well  with the listening portion of toeic this  is not surprising  most people spend more time reading and  not enough time listening  in my own language learning i listen  three times as much as i read  and  both the reading and the listening when  combined start to make you familiar with  the language the language starts to  become your own the brain gets used to  it so that at some point you initiate  speaking and the better you are the  better you understand the greater the  vocabulary the better you're going to do  however  to answer one question when you start  speaking whenever that is you are going  to have difficulty you are going to have  difficulty however if you have a large  vocabulary and you understand well you  can engage in more meaningful  conversations on more meaningful  subjects and so you will progress more  quickly  but you still have to continue with  listening listening and reading and i  can consider them almost the same in a  way because when you are reading very  often when we're reading in a foreign  language we are vocalizing the foreign  language but when we're reading we see  the word so it's easier when we listen  without seeing the words it's more  difficult so you have to put a lot of  effort into listening okay now  the other reason i wanted to  talk about the subject  is no before i leave japan and i'll get  on to japan like in japan  when you google for or look up words  that people search for related to  english learning ego kaiwa english  conversation is a big one somehow people  think that if they have lots of english  conversation they're going to improve  their speaking  i don't believe that  unless you are immersed with native  speakers  but very often the ego kaiwa is five or  six or ten japanese people sitting  around with maybe one native speaker  most of what they're listening to is  other japanese people that's not very  helpful i remember my first month in  japan  i went to a language school it was under  tokyo tower  run by a gentleman i think his name was  mr nito guri he toguri sensei  and his big slogan was koko roto kokoro  heart to heart and we would all sit  around and try and stumble out you know  mumble say you know something in  japanese  i was committed for a month i stayed for  a month and i got out of there because i  would far rather listen to  japanese spoken by a native speaker  i didn't want to hear americans or  frenchmen  you know butchering the japanese  language i wanted to hear the language i  wanted to listen to the language read  the language build up my capability in  the language and then i can interact  with japanese people and improve quickly  so now there was a comment here from  someone whose name was  i can't remember  matt  sharon i don't know  so he says  you know there's a lot to unpack here  and then he  you know tells us what his uh you know  degrees are relating to linguistics and  english teaching credentials okay i'm  not necessarily impressed because and  he's arguing that that you have to start  speaking from the beginning for reasons  that i'll explain here but there are  lots of people who have similar  credentials who don't agree with that  such as steven crash and so the  credentials is kind of irrelevant to me  uh he just also teach music and you  can't learn  to play music or drums if you don't  actually drum okay  you can't learn to play music if you've  never heard music okay so i'm not sure  the music applies to language in any  case but certainly if you have never  heard a song it's more difficult to  learn to play it so i think the  listening analogy does apply but then he  goes on to say meaningful input is  important blah blah blah but you have to  speak to notice your own abilities and  shortcomings it gives you a better  opportunity to improve by targeting  problem areas all of this is true but it  doesn't matter when you do it okay you  can build up quite a lot of potential in  the language  and then start speaking so when you  start to speak it should be  entirely up to you  if you are in a situation where looking  at your classmate unsure of what to say  or what you should be doing then either  you didn't understand the exercise or  the teacher isn't going about the lesson  properly how about we're not in a  classroom how about i'm not dealing with  my classmates i'm just learning on my  own which i think is more intensive  interaction with the language  and then he finally he ends up by saying  that you know i gave i can't remember he  refers to it steve's greek restaurant  story  being pushed to produce he was forced to  use the higher level processing  necessary blah blah blah yeah i think i  went to a restaurant here in vancouver  tried to say something in greek found i  wasn't able to say anything quickly  retreated and didn't attempt to speak  the language again  for at least several months and then  before going to greece i did talk to  people on italki quite a bit and i did  progress quite a bit when i was ready  for it when i felt like doing it  so he then says  that uh yeah at any rate he's saying you  should speak from the beginning because  it identifies your gaps and so forth and  so on of course speaking identifies your  gaps but you don't have to do it from  the beginning what you really have to do  in my opinion is focus on reading and  listening get it in you  and if at an early stage you engage in  conversation especially with other  non-native speakers such as in a  classroom you're wasting your time  and because you're going to pick up the  pronunciation and the errors that the  your fellow classmates have which you  don't want you want to get like when i  listen now to my arabic it's becoming  more and more familiar more and more  comfortable more and more a part of me  and even with regard to pronunciation he  says at the very beginning you should  attempt to pronounce et cetera et cetera  i find that at the very beginning before  i have listened to a lot of the language  i cannot hear  the  vowel sounds clearly the different  aspects of the pronunciation you know i  mentioned that i once got on a forum  when i was starting to learn russian and  i thought i was really quite cool that i  could say some things in russian and i  put up a recording of my spell myself  speaking russian i got roundly  criticized i couldn't really hear it  properly  six months later when i attempt to speak  i've got a better sense of the  intonation better sense of the  pronunciation so  once again if you want to speak better  if you want to speak better make sure  you  listen more like those  those toeic  people in their test  the typically their vocabulary level  corresponds to a corresponds very  closely to the results on the written  test but on the uh  listening test the correlation is less  strong because most people don't listen  enough so listen a lot listen more than  you read focus on input don't worry too  much about when you start speaking  recognize that whenever you start  speaking  you're going to struggle and that  ultimately to speak really well you're  going to have to speak a lot but even as  you up your you know the increased  amount of speaking you're doing continue  with your input  reading and listening activities all  right there you have it and i will if i  can remember what i said i think i'll  say the same in japanese bye for now  [Music]  you

